# Every namcap finding this package accepts, one Python regular expression per
# line, `#` comments and blank lines ignored.  The analogue of
# debian/fuckwayland.lintian-overrides and packaging/rpm/fuckwayland.rpmlintrc.
#
# tests/test_release_pkgbuild.py runs namcap over the built package in CI and
# fails on any line no pattern here matches; tests/test_pkgbuild.py checks
# here, on any box, that every pattern compiles and carries a reason.  namcap
# on the PKGBUILD itself must print nothing at all -- there is no filter for
# that half, and the recon proved the check is live by breaking `license=()`
# and watching "E: Missing license" appear (namcap 3.6.0-3,
# [recon2/pkg-arch.md 3]).
#
# There was a fourth line here until HEAD 375d815: while the tree had no
# LICENSE the recipe said license=('LicenseRef-none') and namcap answered
# "Uncommon license identifiers such as 'LicenseRef-none' require license files
# below /usr/share/licenses/fuckwayland/ ... Found 0/1" (measured verbatim).
# BSD-2-Clause with the file installed answers it, so the pattern went rather
# than staying on as a filter nobody needs.

# warandr is the one tool of six that imports gi, and it does so inside main()
# behind a message that names the packages to install.  namcap's rule is about
# hard imports; making glib2 a depends would put GTK on every sway box for one
# optional GUI, which is exactly the trade the .deb gets wrong (its GTK stack
# is a Depends; tests/test_release_deb.py carries that as an expectedFailure).
# So python-gobject and gtk3 stay optdepends and this finding is accepted.
^fuckwayland E: Dependency glib2 detected and not included

# packaging/common/enable-bridge is a /bin/sh script; namcap reads the
# interpreter as a "referenced library".  bash and sh are in base.
^fuckwayland W: Referenced library 'sh'

# namcap resolves the package's OWN modules against installed packages, so
# every module one of the six console scripts imports is reported as an
# uninstalled dependency (~90 lines on the hand-assembled package).  Whether a
# real makepkg build still produces them is what the first CI run answers; the
# pattern is here so that the answer is a green job either way, and it is
# deliberately narrow -- only our own seven top-level names match.
^fuckwayland W: Referenced python module '(fwcommon|wdotool|wwmctl|wxprop|wxrandr|warandr|wmirror)[.']
